087890625, -0.0009274482727050781, 0.003063201904296875, 0.00196075439453125, 0.0255279541015625, -0.038360595703125, -0.01271820068359375, -0.028289794921875, 0.021728515625, -0.035552978515625, -0.02740478515625, 0.031951904296875, -0.00936126708984375, -0.01458740234375, 0.0200347900390625, 0.01947021484375, 0.036651611328125, 0.01593017578125, -0.0284576416015625, -0.019073486328125, -0.00870513916015625, -0.0112457275390625, 0.011444091796875, 0.00460052490234375, -0.00630950927734375, -0.01116943359375, 0.001895904541015625, 0.0041351318359375, 0.010955810546875, 0.0034160614013671875, -0.001018524169921875, -0.00800323486328125, 0.0322265625, -0.01233673095703125, -0.00557708740234375, 0.0003898143768310547, 0.0131683349609375, -0.0036792755126953125, 0.011566162109375, 0.010894775390625, -0.00997161865234375, -0.0261993408203125, -0.0048828125, 0.0062408447265625, -0.024017333984375, -0.010528564453125, 0.0174407958984375, -0.0233612060546875, 0.02203369140625, 0.007110595703125, 0.026947021484375, 0.0093536376953125, -0.00988006591796875, 0.0006737709045410156, -0.006847381591796875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**Methods**\n\n**1. Symbolic Pattern Construction and Tie Encoding**\n\n*1.1 Algorithmic Overview*  \nAt each time point, we analyze each oscillator\u2019s instantaneous phase (denoted \u03b8i). Using the network adjacency (the set of spatial neighbors for each node), we create a \"symbolic ordinal pattern\" by ranking \u03b8i relative to its neighbors. This results in an ordered sequence that represents the phase configuration in the local neighborhood.\n\n- *Tie Handling*: If the absolute phase difference between any two elements is below a threshold value (\u03b4\u03b8), these are considered \"tied\" and marked with an \u2018=\u2019 in the ordinal encoding, indicating indistinguishable phase values within noise limits.\n  - *Example*: For three phases [\u03b81, \u03b82, \u03b83] where \u03b81 < \u03b82 \u2248 \u03b83 and |\u03b82\u2013\u03b83| < \u03b4\u03b8, the pattern is represented as '1=2' (i.e., \u03b81 < \u03b82 = \u03b83).\n\n*1.2 Rationale and Interpretation*  \nIncluding tie symbols allows us to mark spatial phase clusters\u2014groups of oscillators sharing nearly identical phases (phase plateaus or local synchrony).  \n- *Types of Patterns*:\n    - **Strictly ordered**: e.g., \u20181 2 3\u2019 (monotonic pattern, as in traveling waves or gradients)\n    - **Tied/Plateau**: e.g., \u20181=2\u2019 (signals a phase plateau)\n    - **Mixed/local clusters**: e.g., \u20181 2=3\u2019 (combines ordered and tied elements)\n\nThis richer pattern vocabulary enables quantitative identification of traveling waves (strict order), local synchrony (plateaus/ties), and more complex motifs. The observed frequency of each unique symbolic pattern is used to compute \"spatial permutation entropy,\" which measures the local complexity or disorder in phase arrangement by calculating the Shannon entropy over all patterns in the data.\n\n**2. Phase Difference Threshold (\u03b4\u03b8): Selection and Rationale**\n\n*2.1 Parameter Sweep and Empirical Results*  \nThe value of \u03b4\u03b8 (the phase tie threshold) controls sensitivity to small phase differences. To determine a principled value, we performed parameter sweeps across \u03b4\u03b8 \u2208 [0.05, 0.2] radians, evaluating:\n\n- (1) The variability (variance) of spatial permutation entropy within known dynamical regimes (e.g., synchrony, chimera, traveling wave)\n- (2) The balanced classification accuracy for regime identification, using entropy as a feature for random forest classifiers (implemented with sklearn 1.1.1, 100 trees, max_depth=8, default parameters).\n\n*Summary of Findings*:\n- In synthetic data, regime classification accuracy peaked at \u03b4\u03b8 = 0.1 radians (accuracy 0.92, 95% CI: [0.89, 0.95], effective for chimera detection). Performance dropped sharply for \u03b4\u03b8 outside 0.08\u20130.13 radians.\n- In EEG data, classification accuracy was highest and stable for \u03b4\u03b8 in the range 0.09\u20130.12 radians (accuracy 0.79, 95% CI: [0.76, 0.83]).\n- Minimum within-regime entropy variance also occurred at \u03b4\u03b8 = 0.1 radians for both data types.\n- These results are visualized in Supplementary Figure 2A-B.\n\n*2.2 Conceptual and Mathematical Justification*  \nThe tie threshold \u03b4\u03b8 sets the angular window under which phase differences become indistinguishable. This balances:\n- *Noise robustness*: Larger \u03b4\u03b8 absorbs fluctuations due to measurement noise by grouping similar phases.\n- *Pattern sensitivity*: Smaller \u03b4\u03b8 allows finer discrimination but increases sensitivity to noise.\n\nA practical guideline is to set \u03b4\u03b8 at or above the estimated standard deviation of noise-driven phase fluctuations. While theoretical bounds depend on the phase estimate precision and physical properties of the system, we recommend empirically plotting entropy variance and classification accuracy versus \u03b4\u03b8 to select an appropriate threshold in new contexts.\n\n**3. Neighborhood Definition and Parameterization**\n\n*3.1 Synthetic Data: Rings and Grids*  \n- *Neighborhood*: For ring (1D) and grid (2D) topologies, each node\u2019s neighborhood consists of its k nearest spatial neighbors. By default, k = 2 for rings (immediate left and right), and k = 4 for grids (top, bottom, left, right).\n- *Border Nodes*: At network edges, nodes include only existing neighbors (no periodic wrapping unless specified).\n- *Expanding Neighborhood Size (k)*: For k > 2 (e.g., k = 3 or 4), next-nearest neighbors are included. Border handling remains as above.\n\n*3.2 EEG Data*  \n- *Physical Layout*: For standard 10-20 electrode arrays, each channel\u2019s neighbors are those physically adjacent based on cap geometry.\n- *Missing Channels/Borders*: If a neighbor is missing, only available adjacent electrodes are included, with no artificial links.\n- *High-Density/Irregular Layouts*: For caps deviating from 10-20 geometry, neighbors are assigned as all electrodes within a 4-cm radius based on 3D coordinate positions (matching standard spatial density, typically yielding 3\u20135 neighbors per channel).\n- *Worked Example*: In a 32-channel 10-20 array, electrode C3 is neighbored by C1, C5, F3, and CP3 using this method.\n- *Software Tools*: Network construction used MNE-Python 1.2 functions (`find_ch_adjacency`), augmented with custom scripts (available at [https://github.com/example-repo/spatialentropy]) to compute adjacency matrices from digitized electrode coordinates.\n\n*3.3 Choice and Sensitivity of k*  \n- For EEG, k was chosen to match local physical density\u2014k = 3 for 10-20 arrays, scaling up in denser arrays to maintain neighbor proportion under 20%.  \n- Accuracy of regime classification was robust to k variations between 3\u20135 (\u0394accuracy < 0.02; Supplementary Figure 2C), always above 0.79.  \n- In grids/rings, similar robustness was observed.\n\n*3.4 Irregular or Complex Networks*  \n- Neighbor sets were assigned using node coordinate proximity (spatial radius or fixed degree).\n- Neighbor assignments were visually inspected for anatomical plausibility.\n- For graphs with highly variable node degree or sparse connections, we normalized ordinal pattern counts by neighborhood size to ensure comparability of entropy values and avoid spurious entropy drops in low-degree nodes (see \u201cLimitations\u201d below).\n\n**4. Regime Labeling, Cluster Index, and Validation**\n\n*Synthetic Oscillator Simulations*:  \n- 100 Kuramoto oscillators arranged in rings (1D) or 10\u00d710 grids (2D) were simulated, manipulating coupling (0.05\u20131.0), intrinsic frequency spread (\u03c3 = 0.02\u20130.2), and noise (\u03b7 = 0.01\u20130.1) to generate distinct dynamical regimes.\n- Each regime includes 50 simulations of 10,000 steps.\n\n*Regime Definitions*:  \n- **Synchrony**: All oscillators phase-aligned, e.g., global order parameter R > 0.9 and spatial phase SD < 0.1 (sustained over \u2265200 steps). R is the Kuramoto order parameter, R = (1/N) |\u03a3 exp(i\u03b8j)|.\n- **Chimera**: Co-existence of synchronous and asynchronous subgroups. Identified by 0.5 < R < 0.7 and a \u201ccluster index\u201d > 0.3. The cluster index is computed as the fraction of oscillators belonging to the largest spatially contiguous group whose phases differ by less than \u03b4\u03b8 (see below).\n    - *Cluster Index Calculation*: For each time point, sliding over the array, groups of at least three neighbors are counted as a cluster if all pairwise |\u03b8i\u2013\u03b8j| < \u03b4\u03b8; the index is the size of the largest such group divided by N.\n- **Traveling Wave**: R \u2248 0.8, mean local phase difference > 0.15 rad, and a consistent spatial phase gradient (i.e., phase advances smoothly across space).\n\nRegime assignments were independently confirmed by two blinded raters on samples (n = 20/regime, interrater \u03ba = 0.92).\n\n**5. Entropy Computation and Statistical Analysis**\n\n- Permutation entropy (spatial) was computed in overlapping 250-step time windows (50% overlap; 125-step shift).\n- Classification experiments used random forests (100 trees, max_depth=8, balanced class weights). Max depth was determined via preliminary grid search to avoid overfitting and maintain interpretability.\n- Stratified 5-fold cross-validation assigned all windows from each simulation to a single fold, preventing temporal leakage.\n- Receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curves and area under the curve (AUC) values were computed for testing regime detection, reporting 95% CIs via 1,000-fold stratified bootstrapping.\n    - *Multiple Comparisons*: Bonferroni correction was applied to the family of 6 regime pairwise comparisons (p_corrected < 0.0083).\n- For example, mean AUC for chimera detection = 0.93 [0.91, 0.95], p < 0.001. For phase coherence as a control metric, AUC = 0.67 [0.62, 0.72].\n\n**6. Generalizability, Limitations, and Mitigation**\n\n- *Edge/Bordernode Bias*: Nodes with fewer neighbors (network borders or irregular topologies) have a restricted pattern set, reducing local entropy. Normalized entropy calculations, excluding low-degree"}}